  "id": "Compound::DB01001 - Gene::153",
  "verbalization": "Salbutamol, a well-known adrenergic beta-2 agonist, is linked to Adrb1 through a series of molecular interactions that involve the binding of agonists to their respective receptors. The evidence suggests that Salbutamol, along with its analogs like Pirbuterol and Formoterol, all belong to the broader class of adrenergic beta-2 agonists, which bind to the Adrb1 receptor. This binding mechanism is consistent across the different pathways, with each agonist exhibiting a similar interaction with the receptor. The lowest common ancestors of these entities—Salbutamol and its analogs—highlight a shared biological function, reinforcing the notion that they all act through a common receptor pathway. The integration of these pathways underscores the role of Adrb1 in the physiological effects of these compounds, particularly in bronchodilation, and suggests that the relationship between Salbutamol and Adrb1 is rooted in a shared mechanism of action involving the beta-adrenergic receptor.",
